# General Concept

TypeScript is a **superset** of javascript. Basically, is javascript with more things and type to avoid bugs and inconsistency.

# Interfaces

It's similiar with abstract classes, because describe how objects should be created. But, how this is an interface, we cannot implementing nothing, so, it's a contract

# Type

Allow creates types more flexibles as union, intersections, primitive types and so on.

**e.g**

Interface: 

```typescript
interface User{
	name: string;
	age: number;
}
```

Type:

```typescript
type User{
	name: string;
	age: number;
}
```

Both work exactly the same here.

# Core Differences

## 1. Declaration Merge

Interfaces can be automatically merged.

```typescript
interface User {
  id: number;
}

interface User {
  name: string;
}

// Result:
const user: User = {
  id: 1,
  name: "Alice"
};
```

**Type cannot do this.**

## 2. Extending

Interfaces use **extends**

```typescript
interface Animal {
  name: string;
}

interface Dog extends Animal {
  breed: string;
}
```

Type use **intersections**

```typescript
type Animal = {
  name: string;
};

type Dog = Animal & {
  breed: string;
};
```

Both work, just different syntaxes.

## 3. Unions

Type can describe unions. Interfaces cannot

```typescript
type Status = "loading" | "success" | "error";

type Response =
  | { status: "loading" }
  | { status: "success"; data: string }
  | { status: "error"; error: string };
```

## 4. Primitive and Complex Types

Types can describe:

- Primitives
- Unions
- Tuples
- Mapped types
- Conditional types

```typescript
type ID = string | number;

type Tuple = [number, string];

type Nullable<T> = T | null;

type ReadonlyUser = Readonly<User>;
```


