# Central Security Configuration (`SecurityConfig`)

This configuration brings all pieces together: the BCrypt encoder, the database lookup, and the access rules.

 `SecurityConfig.java`

```java
package com.eden.config;

import com.eden.services.CustomUserDetailsService;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import org.springframework.http.HttpMethod; 
import org.springframework.security.authentication.AuthenticationProvider;
import org.springframework.security.authentication.dao.DaoAuthenticationProvider;
import org.springframework.security.config.Customizer;
import org.springframework.security.config.annotation.web.builders.HttpSecurity;
import org.springframework.security.config.annotation.web.configuration.EnableWebSecurity;
import org.springframework.security.crypto.bcrypt.BCryptPasswordEncoder;
import org.springframework.security.crypto.password.PasswordEncoder;
import org.springframework.security.web.SecurityFilterChain;

@Configuration
@EnableWebSecurity
public class SecurityConfig {

    // 1. Define the Password Encoder (BCrypt is the secure standard)
    @Bean
    public PasswordEncoder passwordEncoder() {
        return new BCryptPasswordEncoder();
    }

    // 2. Define the Authentication Provider (The Login Engine)
    @Bean
    public AuthenticationProvider authenticationProvider(CustomUserDetailsService userDetailsService, PasswordEncoder passwordEncoder) {
        DaoAuthenticationProvider provider = new DaoAuthenticationProvider();

         // Uses the BCrypt bean
        provider.setPasswordEncoder(passwordEncoder); 

        // Uses our CustomUserDetailsService
        provider.setUserDetailsService(userDetailsService); 

        return provider;
    }

    // 3. Define the Filter Chain (Access Control)
    @Bean
    public SecurityFilterChain securityFilterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {

        http
                .csrf(csrf -> csrf.disable()) // Disable CSRF for stateless APIs
                .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
                // ALLOW POST /users for unauthenticated users (registration)
                .requestMatchers(HttpMethod.POST, "/users").permitAll()

                // REQUIRE authentication for ALL other requests
                .anyRequest().authenticated()
                )
                // Use HTTP Basic Authentication
                .httpBasic(Customizer.withDefaults());

        return http.build();
    }
}
```


# The Hashing Step in the Service Layer

This final piece of code ensures that when a user registers, their password is never saved in plain text.

 `UserService.java` (Creation Logic)

```java
package com.eden.services;

import com.eden.dto.user.UserRequestDTO;
import com.eden.dto.user.UserResponseDTO;
import com.eden.models.UserModel;
import com.eden.repository.UserRepository;
import org.springframework.security.crypto.password.PasswordEncoder; 
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class UserService {

    private final UserRepository userRepository;
    private final PasswordEncoder passwordEncoder; 

    public UserService(UserRepository userRepository, PasswordEncoder passwordEncoder) {
        this.userRepository = userRepository;
        this.passwordEncoder = passwordEncoder; // Injected BCrypt bean
    }

    public UserResponseDTO create(UserRequestDTO dto) {
        UserModel user = new UserModel();
        // ... (Mapping other fields)

        // CRITICAL: Hash the plain text password before saving
        String hashedPassword = passwordEncoder.encode(dto.password());
        user.setPassword(hashedPassword);

        userRepository.save(user);

        return new UserResponseDTO(user); 
    }
}
```


# The Successful Login Flow (The Sequence)

| **Step**           | **Your Code Responsible**            | **Technical Action**                                                                                                      |
| ------------------ | ------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **1. Request**     | `httpBasic()`                        | The browser/client sends the **Username** and **Password** in the HTTP header for Basic Authentication.                   |
| **2. Main Engine** | `DaoAuthenticationProvider`          | Receives the credentials (in plain text) and begins the validation process, delegating the data lookup.                   |
| **3. Data Lookup** | `CustomUserDetailsService`           | Queries your **`UserRepository`** using the Username provided.                                                            |
| **4. Load Data**   | `UserModel` and `UserPrincipalModel` | Returns the user entity containing the **Encrypted Password Hash** (`$2a$10$...`) and Roles.                              |
| **5. Validation**  | `BCryptPasswordEncoder`              | **Hashes the password received in Step 1** and compares this new hash against the stored hash from the database (Step 4). |
| **6. Success**     | `SecurityFilterChain`                | If the hashes match (comparison is true), the filter allows the request to proceed to the Controller.                     |

**Real world analogy:**

| **Step**           | **Code Component**          | **Real-World Analogy**                                                                                                                    |
| ------------------ | --------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **1. Request**     | `httpBasic()`               | The client **Presents the ID and Verbal Password** to the security guard.                                                                 |
| **2. Main Engine** | `DaoAuthenticationProvider` | The **Security Manager** who receives the credentials and orchestrates the checking process.                                              |
| **3. Data Lookup** | `CustomUserDetailsService`  | The **Records Department** that knows _where_ to retrieve the member's file (the database).                                               |
| **4. Load Data**   | `UserModel`                 | The **Official Member File** containing the **Encrypted Signature (Hash)** and Clearance Level (Role).                                    |
| **5. Validation**  | `BCryptPasswordEncoder`     | The **Signature Validation Machine** which scrambles the verbal password into a signature and compares it to the file, ensuring security. |
| **6. Success**     | `SecurityFilterChain`       | The **Entry Turnstile** that is only released if the validation (Step 5) is successful.                                                   |

# Default vs. Custom Control

| **Feature**            | **Not Using Your Code (Default/In-Memory)**   | **Using Your Code (Custom DB Auth)**                           |
| ---------------------- | --------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Credential Storage** | Hardcoded in config or properties.            | **Hashed (BCrypt)** and stored in the **PostgreSQL Database**. |
| **Persistence**        | None (Lost on restart).                       | **Fully persistent and dynamic.**                              |
| **Security Standard**  | Low (Often plain text or weak hashing).       | **High (Industry-standard BCrypt).**                           |
| **User Management**    | Manual configuration and application restart. | Via API (`POST /users`) or direct DB access.                   |

